# letibot's guest layer — what a VM needs to build THIS repository.
#
# The cookbook names this file as owed: "a firecode.layer for this project waiting to be written
# (llama.cpp fork, libsqlite3-dev, the Rust toolchain), and until it exists every Rust run in a VM
# pays ten minutes to rediscover it." Three probes paid it on 2026-10-10; this is the receipt.
#
# Applied by scripts/apply-layer.sh --project <dir> BEFORE anybody is told the VM is up, once per
# hash: an unchanged file costs one exec and runs nothing. A line that fails names itself, leaves no
# stamp, and is retried at the next boot — and it does NOT kill the VM, which is what makes a wrong
# guess here cheap and self-correcting rather than fatal. No FROM and no COPY: the base is the
# firecode image, and the project is already mounted at the same absolute path, so a COPY would be a
# second and staler way to say what a path already says.

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# ── The toolchain, made reachable where it is looked for ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-10, and the first probe got this wrong in an instructive way: the image carries a
# Rust toolchain, but in ROOT'S HOME. A probe running as the guest's uid 1000 reported "no toolchain
# anywhere in the VM" — truthfully, of the user it ran as: `command -v cargo` was empty, `$HOME/.cargo`
# did not exist, and `/root/.cargo` answered Permission denied. A later probe with root found
# `cargo 1.98.0` immediately.
#
# So this is DISCOVERY rather than an assertion — and MEASURED, on the second attempt, that a fixed list
# of candidate paths is not discovery either. The first version walked three paths it had guessed and
# found none of them: `/usr/local/bin/cargo` does not exist, `/opt/rust` does not exist, and
# `/root/.cargo` exists but is unreadable to the uid a probe runs as. The loop found nothing, the
# `cargo --version` beneath it failed, and the layer stopped at its FIRST line — leaving the VM up and
# unprovisioned, which is this design working and the reason the failure was legible at all.
#
# So: SEARCH for what the image has, and if it has none, INSTALL one — system-wide, under /usr/local,
# because the layer runs as root and a toolchain in root's home is invisible to the uid the work runs
# as. Rustup's own documented recipe, with RUSTUP_HOME and CARGO_HOME moved out of $HOME.
RUN set -eux; \
    found=""; \
    command -v cargo >/dev/null 2>&1 && found=$(command -v cargo); \
    if [ -z "$found" ]; then \
      found=$(find / -xdev -maxdepth 6 -type f -name cargo -perm -u+x 2>/dev/null | head -1); \
    fi; \
    if [ -n "$found" ]; then \
      ln -sfn "$found" /usr/local/bin/cargo; \
      ln -sfn "$(dirname "$found")/rustc" /usr/local/bin/rustc 2>/dev/null || true; \
    else \
      export RUSTUP_HOME=/usr/local/rustup CARGO_HOME=/usr/local/cargo; \
      curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | \
        sh -s -- -y --no-modify-path --default-toolchain stable; \
      ln -sfn /usr/local/cargo/bin/cargo /usr/local/bin/cargo; \
      ln -sfn /usr/local/cargo/bin/rustc /usr/local/bin/rustc; \
      ln -sfn /usr/local/cargo/bin/rustup /usr/local/bin/rustup; \
    fi; \
    cargo --version; \
    rustc --version

# ── What the build needs from apt ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# libsqlite3-dev is the cookbook's own word for it: the guest has no libsqlite3.so dev symlink, so a
# crate's build script fails. pkg-config and cc are cheap and are what such a script looks for.
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends libsqlite3-dev pkg-config cc; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# ── The llama.cpp checkout crates/llama/build.rs insists on ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# The FORK — `deadtrickster/llama.cpp` — because letibot-llama links that one's libllama. Cloned to
# the absolute path the build reads by default, which is why no LETIBOT_LLAMA_DIR is set anywhere
# here: the path IS the default, and recreating it is a smaller thing to say than an override.
#
# `/home/dead/Projects` does not exist in the guest at all (measured), so the mkdir is load-bearing.
#
# **GUARDED, because a layer's lines must be RE-RUNNABLE.** The stamp is per FILE, not per line: any
# edit to this file re-runs EVERY line below the change, so a bare `git clone` would fail on the
# second apply and take the whole layer down with it — the exact failure the stamp discipline exists
# to make visible, arriving for a reason that has nothing to do with the change being made.
RUN set -eux; \
    mkdir -p /home/dead/Projects; \
    if [ ! -d /home/dead/Projects/llama.cpp/.git ]; then \
      git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/deadtrickster/llama.cpp.git /home/dead/Projects/llama.cpp; \
    fi; \
    test -f /home/dead/Projects/llama.cpp/include/llama.h

# ── The real library, built once and cached ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This was a LINK-ONLY stub in the first draft, and the operator's correction is why it is not:
# **"and firecode caches layers."** The stub was justified by the cost of a build — and a cached
# layer pays that cost ONCE, on the first boot, and then every VM over this project has it. With the
# cost no longer per-VM, the stub buys nothing and gives up something real: a reviewer that can only
# run the tests which do not call into llama, and a build that would deny the whole repository to
# anything that does.
#
# CPU-only, and deliberately so — the guest has no GPU to pass through, GGML_CUDA would be a
# toolchain nobody here has, and a reviewer is checking whether a branch COMPILES AND PASSES, not how
# fast it infers. Tests, examples and the server are off because nothing under test runs them.
#
# Idempotent: cmake re-uses `build-glm`'s own cache and --build re-links only what changed, so a
# re-run is cheap rather than repeated work.
RUN set -eux; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends cmake ninja-build; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
RUN set -eux; \
    cd /home/dead/Projects/llama.cpp; \
    cmake -B build-glm -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DGGML_CUDA=OFF -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF \
          -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF; \
    cmake --build build-glm --target llama --parallel "$(nproc)"; \
    ls -l /home/dead/Projects/llama.cpp/build-glm/bin/libllama.so
